WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.640
- We're at a critical stage here.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:02.890
- Yeah.

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:03.833
You're gonna die.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:05.930
Pretty soon.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.707
- Today, we're watching Family by the Ton.

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:10.390
I'm not gonna lie, not
a huge fan of the title

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:11.846
but let's see what the show has to offer

00:00:11.846 --> 00:00:13.990
and learn something along in the process.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.530
Let's get started.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.300
- I would just eat until
I'm dead, probably.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.910
A normal day for me is wake up around 12,

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.350
figure out something I'm
gonna eat immediately.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.730
TV, video games, bed.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.900
- So it's interesting that
you see him drying himself

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.830
off with a towel of that specific way.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:31.663
When you have excessive body weight

00:00:31.663 --> 00:00:34.410
and you have excess
skin, excess fat tissue,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.270
you have to be really
thorough about drying yourself

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.990
because it's really easy for
a candida infection to begin.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.370
And as a result, you can
get really bad redness,

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:44.720
irritation of the skin.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.930
It can then become a full on cellulitis

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.700
where bacterial infection sets in.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.280
So it's really important to dry skin folds

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.510
in order to decrease the
likelihood of this happening.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.400
- I never would have thought at 34,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.540
I'd be living with my father.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.660
And I'd be, have no job,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.050
have no real money and just be
playing video games all day.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:04.883
And eating.

00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:06.950
- When you're trying to
address someone's situation

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.660
where they're morbidly obese,

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.420
you have to look at
the patient as a whole.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.240
You have to look at what are the reasons

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.730
that contributed to this occurring.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.740
What can you do in order to
prevent it from recurring?

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:17.690
Look at triggers.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.370
Look at mental health factors.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:20.820
Look at sleep schedules.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:21.930
Daily activities.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.790
Like in general, I'm not against gaming.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:24.623
I love gaming.

00:01:24.623 --> 00:01:26.790
I consider myself a
gamer, but there are times

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.160
when we can take something

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.970
that can be potentially beneficial

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.480
and make it into something that it's not.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:32.950
And this can happen with anything.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.890
I mean, it's really the
dose decides the poison.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.380
So having an unhealthy relationship
with food, with gaming,

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.020
all of this can contribute

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.930
to this ultimate unhealthy lifestyle

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.360
which will ultimately decrease lifespan

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:45.740
and decreased quality of life.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.730
And that quality of life is
really what I like to focus on.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.270
'Cause yes, we wanna extend life span.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.610
We want patients to live a long
life, but at the same time,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.770
I want them to live that life comfortably.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.290
I don't want them to be in pain.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.030
I don't want them to be in mental anguish

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:58.863
because they don't feel like

00:01:58.863 --> 00:02:00.260
they're connecting with the outside world.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.760
And that's what a good doctor,
nutritionist, or like I said

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.640
any other health professional looks at

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.001
when addressing these types of problems.

00:02:06.001 --> 00:02:08.300
- Casey, I have your food.
- Okay.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.460
- I'm gonna tell him what to get him

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:10.803
and I'll come right back in a long time,

00:02:10.803 --> 00:02:13.030
just in case he has too much food.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.330
But I'll get it for him and bring it

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.770
to the house and put it
right in front of him.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.400
- [Casey] I don't wanna call
him an enabler, but I mean,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.650
sometimes it's really the way

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.853
like it seems that it is.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.610
I'm not buying the groceries.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.660
I'm not buying the frozen pizzas.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.373
I'm not buying the chicken wings.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:34.000
There's someone facilitating
the food coming in.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:34.920
And it's not me.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.550
- I mean, there's partial truth in that.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.410
I don't think that's a valid
excuse for someone to make.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.569
If my patient told me, hey,
look, it's not about me.

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:43.140
I'm not the one doing the shopping.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:44.550
At the end of the day, there's a level

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.580
of personal responsibility
involved in all of this.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:47.840
But to solely placed the blame

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.700
at the foot of others is
not a healthy pattern.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.590
Not because it's right or wrong

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.370
but because it takes
away control from you.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:56.950
And when you take away control from you,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.860
you actually create more of a problem.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.110
Where you feel like you're helpless,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.140
you have to do it.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.120
And there's nothing you
can do to change it.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.163
When that is not in fact reality.

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:06.370
There are things you can do to change it.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.200
There are conversations to be had

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.300
with your 'enablers" because the foods

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.250
that are being consumed
are A, unhealthy foods.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:16.890
B, there's salt being added
to already very salty foods.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.251
The portion sizes are tremendous.

00:03:18.251 --> 00:03:21.060
And I saw the father mentioned tea.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.860
That tea has so much excess calories

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:25.280
from the added refined sugars.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.640
Like that's even worse

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.170
than eating a huge amount of food

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.330
because at least when you eat the food,

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.420
you can feel satiated.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.390
And there's some fiber and
other nutrients in there.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.380
But when you're just drinking sugar water,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.840
which is essentially what a
lot of these sweet teas are,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.233
in mega amounts, you're
just adding weight.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.170
- So I bathe outside of
this trough currently

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.770
because I cannot physically bathe

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.132
in a sit-down bathtub
or stand up shower area.

00:03:51.132 --> 00:03:54.550
Because I'm a bigger guy
with like folds and flaps,

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:58.870
I have to move around, like
almost like a pig in a way

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:02.780
and wallow and roll over to
get like the back of my leg.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:06.150
I have to lift up literal
like pieces of skin.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.070
It's just a super difficult process.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.800
- There's a lot of
adjustments that are needed

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.320
to be made in someone's home

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:12.880
when they are of this excessive weight.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.380
There's also adjustments
that need to be made

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:15.213
in the hospital.

00:04:15.213 --> 00:04:17.860
I've had patients who are morbidly obese

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.130
who unfortunately did not fit

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.630
in our CAT scan machines
and our MRI machines.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.300
And then those scenarios,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.890
A, you try and look at
the facilities around you

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.450
if there's an option
to transfer the patient

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.320
to someone does have a
larger piece of equipment.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.270
And then there's sometimes been situations

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.460
who we literally have
to contact our local zoo

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.580
who have really large machinery

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:39.310
for some of their larger
animals and we use those.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:41.880
It's not a pleasant conversation
to have with patients

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.810
but it goes to show how there's
a lot that needs to be done

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:46.760
in order to make sure

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.910
that we're keeping
folks like this healthy.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:51.960
- This is our icebreaker.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.624
We do have to take your weight first.

00:04:53.624 --> 00:04:54.900
- This was coming.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.940
When I weigh myself in my
house, I do it butt naked.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.960
Just use the bath in
number one, number two.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.150
And I've like tried to
throw up the last meal I ate

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.230
so I can lose any pounds.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.070
Now I'm there wearing clothes.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:08.880
I'm kicking off my flip flops.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.210
Anything I can get off of
my body to lower the number.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.360
'Cause I know it's
gonna be shockingly big.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.570
- [Woman] It's 711.8.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:23.460
- Now I know bariatric offices that try

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.010
and save this step for later in the visit.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:30.434
Reason being is whether this
patient is approximately 500,

00:05:30.434 --> 00:05:33.940
600, 700 pounds, we know that
there's gonna be a benefit

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:34.773
to doing the procedure.

00:05:34.773 --> 00:05:37.490
So we can already lead
with that assumption.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:39.350
A lot of times it's traumatic to step

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.320
on this scale and look at the number.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.440
So we try and perhaps save it

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.450
for not the first first
thing you do when you walk

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.130
into the office 'cause that alone

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.640
can cause people to not wanna come in

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.110
to see the doctor in the first place.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.487
- We're at a critical stage here.

00:05:51.487 --> 00:05:52.990
- Yeah.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:53.940
- You're gonna die.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:55.813
Pretty soon.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.300
Unless something happens here.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.560
- Now I know that's jarring to hear.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.733
I understand why.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.200
I agree with this approach.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.900
I think this is the
most successful approach

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:06.860
'cause it's honest.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.900
Whether you have a patient
who is morbidly obese

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:12.130
and is over 700 pounds
or you're recommending

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.940
a do not resuscitate
order for a family member.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.990
Those are difficult conversations.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:20.010
The truest, most honest,
non-judgemental way

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.200
you could deliver that news

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.590
is gonna be the most successful way

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:23.720
you could deliver that news.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.420
- With you, the route
we're gonna go down towards

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:27.770
is a sleeve gastrectomy.

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.030
Okay, all right?

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:32.333
So this is your stomach.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:34.243
All right.

00:06:34.243 --> 00:06:36.510
What we're gonna do is we're gonna go in

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.470
and we're gonna remove this outer portion

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.120
of your stomach right here.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.080
There'll be a metabolic
switch that gets flipped

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:43.910
in your body when you have this surgery.

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:45.670
Your body will wanna lose weight.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.960
- When Dr. Proctor was
going over to surgery,

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:52.480
it was pretty scary only because
I fear surgery in general.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.460
- Understandable that he's apprehensive.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.960
Surgery comes with
legitimate, legitimate risks.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.420
Even if it's bariatric surgery.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.240
That being said, being 700 plus pounds

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.320
carries way more risks than the surgery.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.420
And that's how we decide
whether or not a procedure,

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.540
a medication, a treatment
is good for a patient.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.270
- Why don't you tell me
what does a normal day

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.610
of eating look like for you

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:12.290
prior to when you saw Dr. Proctor?

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.763
- So like, we're waking up at like 12.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:19.050
And then probably ordering
food from the hot dog house.

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:20.870
- What do you usually order?
- On like a normal day,

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.610
it might be like 20
wings or like 10 tenders.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.150
And then it would be like a small order

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:27.726
of tater tots and a large sweet tea.

00:07:27.726 --> 00:07:28.610
- This size cup?

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:29.820
- Yeah.
- Okay.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.890
So we're doing 32 ounces of sweet tea.

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:36.010
- My lunches were either a
taco salad or like tacos.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.550
But it was, you know,
not a lot of sour cream.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:39.480
And then the rest of it.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:40.313
- Then what do you have in dinner?

00:07:40.313 --> 00:07:41.146
- It could be anything

00:07:41.146 --> 00:07:43.000
from like a big thing of fried chicken

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.340
or pizza we'd have like, not
occasionally but sometimes.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.240
- So Dr. Proctor wants
you to lose 60 pounds

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:50.770
by the time he sees you next.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.140
- The reason why all of
these guidelines are in place

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:55.320
about having patients lose
a certain amount of weight

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.510
before the surgery, having
them see a psychologist,

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.680
running blood tests is you wanna set

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.020
the patient up for success.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:04.310
If we just willy nilly
did bariatric surgeries

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:07.260
on everybody who is obese,
it would be problematic

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.040
because what happens is
when you do the surgery,

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.260
there's very specific
protocol you have to follow.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:14.470
If you overeat with a smaller stomach,

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.640
you can actually cause surgical issues.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:17.930
You can rupture your stomach.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.360
You can cause vomiting.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:20.970
It can cause mental havoc.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.510
It can lead you to going
into a dark depression.

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.780
We really try and select our patients

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.370
that we think are gonna
have the best outcome

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.050
from the bariatric surgery

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:32.650
instead of just recommending it for all.

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:34.140
- So I've hired a personal trainer

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.490
for the first time in my life.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.560
This is the first time I've
been to the gym in a long time.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.470
And I am a little bit nervous.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:41.303
- Hey, what's up man?

00:08:41.303 --> 00:08:42.136
- Casey, right?

00:08:42.136 --> 00:08:43.400
- Yeah, yeah, Dex?
- Yes, sir.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:46.070
- When I watch programs like this on TLC,

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:47.610
my one issue that I have

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:51.360
is this is a lot of this is
not reality for most Americans.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.640
I don't want someone who
is potentially wanting

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:54.900
to lose weight to look at this

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.520
and think that unless I have
this, I can't lose weight.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.590
It's not true.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:02.970
You don't need the diesel personal trainer

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.420
in order to lose the weight.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:05.880
Any kind of physical activity

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.890
when you're going from zero will help.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.620
Also, being kind to yourself

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.560
and not pushing yourself too hard

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:13.500
in the beginning is also really important.

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.990
With someone who is 700 plus pounds,

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.680
you really wanna make
sure that you're focusing

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:18.723
on injury prevention.

00:09:18.723 --> 00:09:22.770
It's very easy to overload
joints, to pull and hurt muscles.

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:25.440
And as a result, you can set someone back.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.210
So it's really about starting slow

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.080
- Do you arm rotation.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:31.550
- All right, gotcha.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:32.383
- Good.

00:09:32.383 --> 00:09:33.553
And we'll go back the opposite way.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.190
I like Casey to move his own body weight

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:38.250
so he can establish a correlation

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.640
between what he's accomplished already

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.140
and when he can expect
himself to do in the future.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.300
- See, someone might look at that and say,

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:45.133
that's not exercise.

00:09:45.133 --> 00:09:45.966
Yes, it is.

00:09:45.966 --> 00:09:46.799
His heart rate's going up,

00:09:46.799 --> 00:09:47.750
his breathing rate's going up.

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:50.660
This all automatically
kicks up the metabolism,

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:52.610
helps them prepare to meet the challenge.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:53.700
And he's been sedentary.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.430
You can't push too hard, too fast.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:57.070
You can put too much strain on the heart

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.410
and even cause a heart attack.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.090
There's actually a type of heart attack

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:02.170
called the type II myocardial infarction.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:05.150
That happens as a result
of not a pure blockage,

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:06.990
but simply because
there was so much strain

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.490
on the heart that there wasn't
enough circulation going

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:11.310
that actual damage has occurred.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.210
- It's been three months since
my surgery and I feel good.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.030
Little things are changing in me

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:20.360
that I didn't really expect
to change so quickly.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:24.370
As I accomplish more, my
self-esteem is rising.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:26.020
So I feel like I can do more.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.130
So it's just a good feeling.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:31.950
(crowd cheering and applauding)

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:34.010
I started this journey at 711 pounds

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:37.320
and today, I'm happy to say that I'm 545.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:38.153
- I think it's great

00:10:38.153 --> 00:10:41.010
when you start seeing your goals pan out

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:42.730
and that's how motivation happens.

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:45.280
It's seeing your hard work be paid off

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:47.530
and it might not happen off the
first, second or third time.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:48.810
But as you continue,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:50.770
you keep seeing that
you're making improvements.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:53.070
Whether it's after a week,
after a month or after a year.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:54.780
And even for myself, I had a buddy

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.300
that was in the ICU recently

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.230
and had a lot of neurological deficits.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:10:59.654
And as he was solely recovering,

00:10:59.654 --> 00:11:02.380
he would call me each day upset the fact

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:03.880
that he wasn't making progress.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.320
And I had to remind him.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.900
Write down what your deficits are today.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.580
Do it next week, do it the following week.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:10.910
And then each time you're upset,

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:12.760
look back and see how
much you've improved.

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.050
When you look in every
hour by hour, day by day,

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:16.540
sometimes it's easy to lose sight

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.060
of how much you've accomplished.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.430
But when you really spread
it out and think in scale,

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.550
which we, as humans are not good at doing,

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.347
you get to see that accomplishment.

00:11:24.347 --> 00:11:25.750
You get to feel motivated.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:28.420
You get to see that you're making moves

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:29.253
in the right direction

00:11:29.253 --> 00:11:31.470
even if it's three steps
forward one step back.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.187
- Well, I'm trying to
get my license renewed.

00:11:33.187 --> 00:11:34.783
- This is him now?
- This was last month.

00:11:34.783 --> 00:11:38.082
Because in my other license, I'm huge.

00:11:38.082 --> 00:11:40.291
I'm a whale.

00:11:40.291 --> 00:11:41.580
So there we go.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.140
That's me.

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.790
I don't know if you
can see that real well.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.000
I don't know, but that's me.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.470
It's really weird too,
because like 2020 Corona

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.820
is like the worst year
of some people's lives.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:53.230
Like it's really been awful for people

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:55.800
but like, it's gonna go
down in the record books.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.950
It's like one of the best
years of my life probably.

00:11:57.950 --> 00:11:59.590
Life for me, is blossoming.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:02.130
- What's he's experiencing right now

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.720
is almost like a form of
post-traumatic growth.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.540
It's looking back at your accomplishments.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:07.840
There's nostalgia there.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.390
And the fact that he's
in fact reached his goal.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:13.500
He's in fact, improved
his quality of life.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.910
It goes to show that
everything that we discussed

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:17.460
at the beginning of this
episode, where it comes

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.890
to extending life and
improving quality of life,

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:22.740
he's a walking testimonial
for, really proud of him.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.190
Check out this video of
me taking a career test.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:27.210
Do you think I got
doctor or something else?

